<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna x87depth warnings: a declared type that the x87 stack depth
    contradicts.

        8048100 h:  fld1; ret                 ; leaves one value on the x87 stack
        8048110 g:  call h; fstp st(0); ret   ; pops what h left
        8048120 f:  fld1; ret

    h and f are declared to return int, so their result is read from EAX,
    while the code returns its value in ST0. IDA warns about both shapes
    (WARN_MUST_RET_FP for the function, and a call warning when a callee
    typed otherwise leaves a value). The depth solve already proves that h's
    call leaves one value (g pops it) and that f returns at depth 1.

    Stage: P4 prototype warnings (ActionPrototypeWarnings), option x87depth
    off|on. Pass 1 (off) has no warning, pass 2 (on, the default) both.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x8048100">
d9e8c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x8048110">
e8ebffffffddd8c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x8048120">
d9e8c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x8048100" name="h"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x8048110" name="g"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x8048120" name="f"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern int4 h(void);</com>
  <com>parse line extern int4 f(void);</com>
  <com>option x87depth off</com>
  <com>lo fu g</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option x87depth on</com>
  <com>lo fu g</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="X87DEPTHWARN #1: on, the call to h is flagged" min="1" max="1">call to h leaves a value on the x87 stack</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="X87DEPTHWARN #2: on, f is flagged" min="1" max="1">the function leaves a value on the x87 stack but its return type is not floating-point</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
